{
  // Output filename and BoardLoader data-variable name:
  //   boardName "RP2040" -> designer/boards/RP2040/RP2040.json -> const RP2040Data = {...}
  "boardName": "RP2040",

  // Fallback human-readable name shown in the designer UI.  Overridden
  // by the matching `<id>.name=` entry in a sibling-or-ancestor
  // boards.txt when one is present (matched via `<id>.build.variant=`
  // against this variant's directory name) — for this board that's
  // "Amken Revelop eS" (variants/rp2040/boards.txt), vendor's own
  // product name is "Revelop eS" per tools/json/amken_revelop_es.json
  // in the earlephilhower/arduino-pico repo.
  "displayName": "Raspberry Pi Pico (RP2040)",

  // Same parser dialect as every other RP2040 board — see
  // ../board.json for the full rationale.
  "family": "rp2040",

  // No genuinely input-only pins confirmed on this board — the pins
  // corrected below are ordinary bidirectional GPIOs merely wired to
  // dedicated onboard-chip roles (SPI bus lines, chip-selects, an
  // interrupt line, a NeoPixel + its power gate), handled via chipGpios
  // (emptied/tagged capabilities) rather than inputOnlyGpios.
  "inputOnlyGpios": [],

  // Default transports — Serial only. boards.txt declares no WiFi radio
  // for this board (plain "rp2040" chip bucket; USB VID/PID 0x2E8A/
  // 0x00C0 and 0x2770/0x7306 per tools/json/amken_revelop_es.json, no
  // cyw43 references).
  "connections": ["serial"],

  "pinNotes": {},

  // pfodWeb NOTE (RP2040 family audit, button/LED/NeoPixel tagging +
  // dedicated-hardware pin exclusion, 2026-07-13):
  //
  // Board: Amken "Revelop eS" — an RP2040-based 3D-printer motion-
  // control mainboard from Amken LLC (Amken3D, amken.us / amken3d.com,
  // Springfield VA, USA). Unlike its sibling amken_bunny (audited
  // earlier this session, apparently discontinued/unlisted), "revelop"
  // and "revelop e-S" ARE both listed as Amken's current product lineup
  // on amken3d.com (checked live 2026-07-13): "revelop e-S is small,
  // but mighty. It is robust with all of the must-have features plus
  // extra bells and whistles." Added to earlephilhower/arduino-pico in
  // PR #2283 ("Add Amken boards", merged 2024-07-19, commit
  // 1f71135a2b31350e40370b84c54c3e19f5e7ba55, co-authored by Amken's
  // H.Keni), alongside three siblings: Amken Bunny, Amken Revelop,
  // Amken Revelop Plus. The PR description states these boards were
  // originally designed for Klipper firmware and are being adapted for
  // Marlin via the Arduino-Pico core; neither the PR description/
  // discussion nor the commit contains any hardware description,
  // datasheet, schematic, or pinout diagram.
  //
  // Sources consulted (WebSearch/WebFetch, 2026-07-13):
  //  1. This board's own pins_arduino.h (variants/rp2040/rp2040/
  //     amken_revelop_es/pins_arduino.h) — vendor-authored section
  //     comments and #define groupings are the ONLY source of hardware
  //     identity found for this board (see per-pin reasoning below);
  //     treated as authoritative since it is the actual arduino-pico
  //     board-support source the generated sketches compile against,
  //     and its per-peripheral comment headers ("// LEDs", "// NeoPixel",
  //     "// CAN bus", "//Accelerometer", "//MAX31865", "// Shared
  //     between LIS2D and MAX31865") are vendor labeling, not a guess
  //     from alias names alone. An earlier pfodWeb-session "pfodWeb
  //     NOTE" comment already present in this file (line 59) had already
  //     identified GPIO0-3 (SPI0) and GPIO8-11 (SPI1+CAN_CS) as
  //     dedicated by omitting D0-D3/D8-D11 from the file's static-const
  //     alias block — this audit independently re-derives and confirms
  //     that same conclusion plus additional findings (GPIO5 NeoPixel
  //     tag, GPIO20/24/25/29 chipGpios corrections) that the omission
  //     technique alone cannot express, because those four pins DO carry
  //     a routable static-const alias (D5/D20/D24/A3-D29 respectively)
  //     despite each also being a confirmed dedicated-hardware pin.
  //  2. raw.githubusercontent.com/earlephilhower/arduino-pico/master/
  //     tools/json/amken_revelop_es.json (PlatformIO board descriptor):
  //     confirms vendor name "Amken", product name "Revelop eS", url
  //     "https://www.amken3d.com", USB VID/PID 0x2E8A/0x00C0 and
  //     0x2770/0x7306, 16MB max flash, boot2 "w25q16jvxq" — no
  //     hardware/pin description beyond what pins_arduino.h contains.
  //  3. amken3d.com (official vendor site, live 2026-07-13): a minimal
  //     marketing landing page only ("Revelop 3D Printer Controller
  //     Boards" heading, two short blurbs for "revelop" and "revelop
  //     e-S", a contact-us link) — confirms the product name/identity
  //     and that it is a current (not discontinued) SKU, but contains NO
  //     technical specifications, pinout diagram, schematic, or manual.
  //     Only two links exist on the whole site (home, /contact/); no
  //     shop/docs/GitHub links were found to follow for more detail.
  //  4. github.com/earlephilhower/arduino-pico PR #2283 ("Add Amken
  //     boards") description and review discussion: confirms the four
  //     Amken boards were originally Klipper-firmware boards adapted for
  //     Marlin/Arduino-Pico; contains no pin-level hardware detail for
  //     any of the four boards.
  //  5. github.com/amken3d (org repos: gopper — a TinyGo port of Klipper
  //     firmware targeting RP2040/RP2350/STM32 — and KliPiNP, a Klipper/
  //     OpenPNP adaptation): neither repo's browsable content contains a
  //     "Revelop eS"-specific board profile, pin table, or peripheral
  //     wiring information; gopper documents generic RP2040/RP2350
  //     target support, not this specific board.
  //  6. WebSearch across many query variations ("Amken Revelop eS RP2040
  //     pinout accelerometer MAX31865 CAN", "Revelop e-S Amken specs CAN
  //     bus accelerometer", etc.): no independent product page, forum
  //     thread, review, schematic, or pinout diagram for this specific
  //     board was found anywhere beyond source 1-5 above.
  //
  // Given the practical research ceiling (no schematic/pinout diagram
  // reachable anywhere), this audit relies on pins_arduino.h's own
  // vendor-authored per-peripheral comment groupings as the sole but
  // sufficient confirmation of each dedicated pin's role — consistent
  // with this audit's rule to report NO CHANGE rather than guess: every
  // correction below traces directly to an explicit vendor comment/
  // macro name in this board's own pins_arduino.h, none is inferred
  // from a bare GPIO number or alias name alone.
  //
  // Per-pin findings (full per-source detail in
  // boardsDetails/rp2040/rp2040/amken_revelop_es/notes.txt):
  //   GPIO0 (PIN_SPI0_MISO / MISO) - SPI0 bus, "// Shared between LIS2D
  //     and MAX31865" per the file's own comment - dedicated to the
  //     onboard accelerometer + RTD temperature sensor, not general
  //     purpose. capabilities emptied; build_boards.js's own MISO-alias
  //     match still correctly attaches spi_miso on top (same policy as
  //     the sparkfun_thingplusrp2350 GPIO2/3/4 precedent).
  //   GPIO1 (PIN_SPI0_SS / SS / PIN_LIS_CS) - the LIS2D accelerometer's
  //     own chip-select, doubling as the SPI0 bus's hardware SS line
  //     (both macros resolve to the same GPIO1). Dedicated. capabilities
  //     emptied; spi_ss auto-append still correctly reflects its real
  //     role (matches sparkfun_thingplusrp2350 GPIO9 precedent).
  //   GPIO2 (PIN_SPI0_SCK / SCK) - shared SPI0 clock for both onboard
  //     chips. Dedicated. capabilities emptied; spi_sck auto-append
  //     retained.
  //   GPIO3 (PIN_SPI0_MOSI / MOSI) - shared SPI0 data line. Dedicated.
  //     capabilities emptied; spi_mosi auto-append retained.
  //   GPIO5 (PIN_LED = PIN_NEOPIXEL, both literally "(5u)") - this board
  //     has no separate plain LED; its single onboard WS2812-style
  //     NeoPixel doubles as the board's LED_BUILTIN, exactly the same
  //     pattern as many Pico-derived boards (e.g. Pimoroni Plasma2040,
  //     SparkFun Thing Plus RP2350). Tagged led_neopixel, matching this
  //     repo's established capability list for confirmed onboard
  //     NeoPixels (bigtreetech_SKR_Pico GPIO24, sparkfun_thingplusrp2350
  //     GPIO14): ["digital_output","led_neopixel","pwm_output"].
  //     digital_input dropped - committed to driving the LED.
  //   GPIO20 (NEOPIXEL_POWER) - directly grouped under this file's own
  //     "// NeoPixel" comment header alongside PIN_NEOPIXEL, confirming
  //     it is the NeoPixel's power-enable/gate line - exact same role
  //     and exact same GPIO-numbering pattern (NEOPIXEL_POWER present
  //     alongside PIN_NEOPIXEL) as the real-hardware-documented Adafruit
  //     Feather RP2040 CAN Bus precedent in this repo (adafruit_
  //     feather_can GPIO20, confirmed via Adafruit's own pinouts guide:
  //     "NeoPixel power pin... needs to be set High for the NeoPixel to
  //     be powered"). This board's own pins_arduino.h ALSO separately
  //     defines PIN_SERIAL1_TX as (20u) later in the file (Serial
  //     section) - but PIN_SERIAL1_RX is sentineled to (31u), i.e. not
  //     pinned out at all, so Serial1 cannot function as a real
  //     bidirectional UART on this board regardless; the NeoPixel-power
  //     role is the one directly vendor-labeled under its own dedicated
  //     comment header, so it is treated as this pin's real function.
  //     capabilities emptied (matches adafruit_feather_can GPIO20
  //     precedent exactly - no polarity claim made, purely excluded from
  //     general routable use as a dedicated power-gate line).
  //   GPIO24 (PIN_MAX31865_CS) - "//MAX31865" comment directly above the
  //     macro confirms this is the onboard MAX31865 RTD temperature-
  //     sensor chip's dedicated SPI chip-select. Was ALSO exposed as a
  //     plain D24 general-purpose alias in this file's static-const
  //     block (line "static const uint8_t D24 = (24u);") - a real defect
  //     of the same class as the bigtreetech_SKR_Pico GPIO8/9 TMC-UART
  //     fix and the adafruit_feather_can GPIO13 LED/SS-alias fix: without
  //     this override, the confirmed MAX31865 CS pin would have been
  //     offered as an innocuous general digital I/O pin, even though
  //     driving it from a Designer sketch would corrupt SPI communication
  //     with the onboard temperature sensor. GPIO24 is a separate,
  //     manually-toggled CS line (driven directly by the MAX31865 driver
  //     library), distinct from the shared SPI0 hardware SS pin
  //     (PIN_SPI0_SS/PIN_LIS_CS = GPIO1, see above). capabilities emptied.
  //   GPIO25 (PIN_LIS_INTERRUPT1) - "//Accelerometer" comment block
  //     confirms this is the onboard LIS2D accelerometer's interrupt
  //     output line (the file's own commented-out `//#define
  //     PIN_LIS_INTERRUPT1 (23u)` immediately above shows the vendor
  //     moved this signal from GPIO23 to GPIO25 during board bring-up -
  //     GPIO25 is the currently active assignment, GPIO23 is dead/unused
  //     and left general-purpose below). Was ALSO exposed as plain D25.
  //     Same defect class as GPIO24 - capabilities emptied.
  //   GPIO29 (PIN_CAN_INTERRUPT) - "// CAN bus" comment block confirms
  //     this is the onboard CAN transceiver's interrupt line. Was ALSO
  //     exposed as both D29 and A3 (analog) general-purpose aliases.
  //     Same defect class as GPIO24/25 - capabilities emptied.
  //
  // GPIO8/9/10/11 (SPI1 MISO/CAN_CS/SCK/MOSI, all feeding the onboard
  // CAN transceiver) already have NO static-const alias anywhere in this
  // file (the D-alias block jumps from D7 directly to D12) - already
  // correctly excluded from the Designer's routable pin list by
  // omission, matching this file's own pre-existing pfodWeb NOTE and the
  // amken_bunny GPIO4/5 TMC-UART precedent; no chipGpios override is
  // needed or added for these four pins since there is nothing broken to
  // fix. GPIO23 (dead/unused former PIN_LIS_INTERRUPT1 location, now
  // commented out) is likewise left at full chip-default capability -
  // no active hardware role. GPIO4/6/7/12-19/21/22/26/27/28 are ordinary
  // broken-out GPIOs with no dedicated-hardware macro pointing to them
  // anywhere in this file - left at full chip-default capability. No
  // button of any kind (no PIN_BUTTON alias, no vendor documentation of
  // a physical button beyond the RP2040's own BOOTSEL, out of scope per
  // the rpipico/board.json precedent) - no button tag applied.
  //
  // Full findings/sources trail:
  // boardsDetails/rp2040/rp2040/amken_revelop_es/notes.txt
  "chipGpios": {
    "0":  { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_SPI0_MISO - SPI0 bus shared between the onboard LIS2D accelerometer and MAX31865 RTD temperature sensor ('// Shared between LIS2D and MAX31865' per this board's own pins_arduino.h). Dedicated, not general purpose." },
    "1":  { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_SPI0_SS / PIN_LIS_CS - onboard LIS2D accelerometer's dedicated SPI chip-select, also the SPI0 bus's hardware SS line (same GPIO). Dedicated, not general purpose." },
    "2":  { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_SPI0_SCK - shared SPI0 clock for the onboard LIS2D accelerometer + MAX31865 sensor. Dedicated, not general purpose." },
    "3":  { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_SPI0_MOSI - shared SPI0 data line for the onboard LIS2D accelerometer + MAX31865 sensor. Dedicated, not general purpose." },
    "5":  { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "led_neopixel", "pwm_output"], "note": "PIN_LED = PIN_NEOPIXEL (same GPIO) - this board's single onboard WS2812-style NeoPixel doubles as its LED_BUILTIN; no separate plain LED exists." },
    "20": { "capabilities": [], "note": "NEOPIXEL_POWER - onboard NeoPixel power-enable/gate line, grouped under this board's own '// NeoPixel' comment header alongside PIN_NEOPIXEL. Dedicated, not general purpose. (This GPIO is also assigned PIN_SERIAL1_TX later in the file, but PIN_SERIAL1_RX is sentineled to (31u)/not pinned out, so Serial1 cannot function as a real UART on this board regardless.)" },
    "24": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_MAX31865_CS - onboard MAX31865 RTD temperature-sensor chip's dedicated SPI chip-select ('//MAX31865' comment). Was also exposed as a plain D24 general-purpose alias - corrected here; driving this pin generically would corrupt communication with the onboard sensor." },
    "25": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_LIS_INTERRUPT1 - onboard LIS2D accelerometer's interrupt output line ('//Accelerometer' comment; the vendor's own commented-out prior assignment at GPIO23 confirms GPIO25 is the currently active pin). Was also exposed as a plain D25 general-purpose alias - corrected here." },
    "29": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_CAN_INTERRUPT - onboard CAN transceiver's interrupt line ('// CAN bus' comment). Was also exposed as plain D29/A3 general-purpose aliases - corrected here." }
  },

  // arduino-pico's analogWrite() defaults to an 8-bit range (0-255) —
  // same as every other RP2040/RP2350 board.
  "pwm": { "min": 0, "max": 255, "method": "analogWrite" },

  // No hardware DAC on RP2040/RP2350.
  "dac": {},

  // RP2040's one 12-bit ADC (0-4095), ~3.3V reference — matches this
  // board's own pins_arduino.h A0-A3 = GPIO26-29 declarations exactly.
  "adc": { "bits": 12, "max": 4095, "defaultRefVolts": "3.3" }
}
